+# ADR-002: Versioned events, atomic boundaries and replay
+
+Date: 2026-10-05. Status: PROPOSED — D08/D09/D10 approval required. Design only; no new producer, consumer, collection, table or migration was implemented.
+
+## Existing transports and actual guarantees
+
+| Boundary | Source evidence | Current local contract | Missing or limited guarantee |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Mongo master/metadata → central identities or cache | `api/skus.js:118`, `api/channels.js:49`, `api/sku-channel-links.js:53`, `api/locations.js:24`; independent PG catalog in `inventory/central-api.js:35-48`/`inventory/workbook.js:91` | Dedicated Mongo mutations and independent PostgreSQL metadata operations | No general Mongo transactional outbox, consumer receipt or master revision/identity bridge. |
+| Mongo GRN → PG receipt → Mongo final status | `api/inbound-create-edit.js:234-262`, `api/inbound-realtime.js:219-248`, `inventory/wms-operations.js:99-174` | Staged request/CAS; exact SKU/location/PO quantity validation; PG idempotent ledger; Mongo finalize after PG commit | A process can stop between stores. Existing same-request retries/reconciliation are valuable but do not make both stores atomic; there is no autonomous durable Mongo dispatch contract. |
+| Shopify webhook → PG order/task | `inventory/http.js:40-55`, `inventory/ingress.js:7-24` | Raw-body signature, delivery-ID digest check; transaction inserts `ci_ingress`, order/timeline/alert, webhook evidence and eligible `shopify_ingress` task | `orders/updated` is recorded without stock task; observation-only accounts do not enqueue stock tasks. No shared versioned schema. |
+| Woo webhook → PG order/task | `inventory/http.js:22-38` | Raw-body signature, same-ID/different-body conflict, local transaction; `woocommerce_ingress` task | `product.updated` task is a no-op in `inventory/workers.js`; no general recovery order poll. Terminal physical semantics need D09. |
+| Amazon SQS → PG receipt/task | `inventory/sqs-worker.js:4-13`, `inventory/ingress.js:26-30`, `scripts/inventory-amazon-sqs.mjs` | Receive up to 10, 20-second long poll; accept transaction before delete; PG ingress digest dedupe | The first parse/accept failure aborts the loop, later batch messages are not isolated, CLI has no per-cycle error catch. Source queue policy/DLQ/visibility and subscription are unverified. Render Blueprint does not start this CLI. |
+| Shopify recovery poll → Unified Orders | `inventory/shopify-orders.js:9-35`, `scripts/inventory-worker.mjs` | Every 60 seconds; 5-minute overlap; first lookback 30 days; up to 100 pages × 50 orders; >100-line order aborts observation; each order local transaction; checkpoint after pages | Updates order observations and alerts but emits no reserve/cancel task. Overlap limits some misses; using completion `now()` as checkpoint can still miss late records beyond the overlap. Cursor has failure/backoff state, no jitter here. |
+| Meesho order report → Unified Orders | `inventory/meesho-orders.js:10-52`, `inventory/connections.js:228-231` | Validated columns/time, deterministic line IDs, digest includes mapping/report time, receipt/order/account state transaction | `notify:false`, no ledger effect. File import is not an API live feed. Ordering is report time, not delivery time. |
+| Programme report → parent order association/custody observation | `inventory/sales-programs.js`, `db/migrations/014_sales_programs.sql` | Requires existing parent order/enrollment; time guards, digest dedupe, conflict checks, local transaction | Does not create parent orders or physical stock movements. Full provider contract is owner-supplied/unverified. |
+| Flipkart/other partner order intake | `scripts/flipkart-probe-orders.mjs`; `inventory/connection-observation.js`; `inventory/connection-readiness.js:87-107` | Probe/sample/report evidence only | Flipkart complete order adapter, callback and recovery poll are absent. Probe must not be used as a worker. Offline `api/order-import.js` and `api/common-import.js` business processing returns 501. |
+| Ledger → channel publishing | `inventory/ledger.js:21-35`, `inventory/workers.js:174-250`, `inventory/connection-setup.js:80-99` | Ledger/balance/reservation + `ci_outbox[publish_stock]` share PG transaction. Worker derives latest sellable/pack size under per-mapping advisory lock; activation/reconciliation also enqueue | Tasks have differing unversioned payload shapes; sequence of outbox IDs is not guaranteed aggregate commit order. Shopify compare/set key uses latest balance; Woo readback; Amazon submission is not readback confirmation. Activation API currently permits only Shopify/Woo although publisher has Amazon branch. |
+| Observed channel stock → drift | `inventory/stock-observations.js`; `inventory/shopify-snapshot.js:38-52`; `inventory/workers.js:295-326`; `inventory/connections.js:203-222` | Signed provider-event observation enforces timestamps and same-time conflict; reconciliation rereads fresh balance; provider observations never update physical ledger | Full snapshot/report writers have different ordering rules. Partner `/:id/snapshot` uses receipt `now()` without a provider revision, whereas `inventory/partner-file.js` rejects snapshots lacking an approved ordering contract. This is a real route-specific conflict, not a blanket assertion that all snapshot intake is disabled. |
+| Intake → WhatsApp team alerts | `inventory/unified-orders.js:57-74`, `inventory/order-alert-routing.js`, `inventory/whatsapp.js:98-165` | Order/timeline/source facts/alert intent share caller PG transaction. Sender claims one at a time, verifies consent/policy and sends outside DB transaction | Team partial unique index dedupes one per order/recipient; legacy path can insert both new-order and packing events. Initial packing queue can precede reservations. External acceptance vs local write is not atomic. |
+| Meta status → alert/timeline | `inventory/whatsapp.js:168-216` | Signed full-batch validation, configured tenant and phone-ID binding, provider-message lookup scoped by tenant, monotonic status guards and per-status transaction | No durable webhook inbox/event envelope; unknown send result is quarantined as `uncertain`, not blindly retried. Missing receipts cannot prove non-delivery. |
+| Return QC → ledger | `inventory/return-service.js:17-44` | Return receipt/case/ledger/timeline local transaction; request ID and receipt content checks | No Mongo return projection. A financial refund never proves returned physical goods. |
+| Immediate WMS move → ledger | `inventory/wms-operations.js:53-76` | Two ledger movements in one PG transaction, request-scoped keys | Not a shipped STO or in-transit lifecycle; that path is disabled in `api/sto-editor.js`. |
+| Tally export → held review | `inventory/tally-bridge.js:49-60` | Digest inbox + account verification + encrypted `tally_voucher_review` task status `held` share transaction | No approved order/stock/finance consumer. Native worker does not claim `held` tasks. |
+| PG change → UI refresh | `db/migrations/011_live_inventory_updates.sql`, `inventory/unified-orders.js:137-148`, `src/hooks/useInventoryLiveUpdates.tsx` | Trigger `pg_notify('ci_inventory_changes',tenant)`, SSE `changed {at}`, UI reload on hint; debounce 1.5 seconds and 60-second fallback | NOTIFY/SSE have no durable event ID, per-entity sequence or replay cursor. Notifications are hints only; a GET from the authoritative store provides data. |
+
+## Proposed envelope and schemas
+
+`event-catalog.json` lists 58 versioned events, each with aggregate/key, idempotency expression, typed schema, producer, consumer, retry/ordering policy, current native contract and decision. `event-schemas.json` is a JSON Schema 2020-12 design artifact with 36 payload definitions and one discriminated branch per event; it is NOT wired to any runtime validator.
+
+Required common fields: `event_id` UUID, `event_type` ending `.v1`, `schema_version=1`, `tenant_id`, `aggregate={type,key,sequence}`, `idempotency_key`, `occurred_at`, `recorded_at`, `source={store,producer,source_revision}`, `payload_digest`, `correlation_id`, nullable `causation_id`, and event-specific `payload`. Version 1 disallows unknown payload/envelope properties. Producer canonically hashes the business payload; delivery timestamps, retries and diagnostics are not part of business identity. Event ID remains stable across retries. Idempotency expressions in the catalog represent canonical serialized tuples (not ambiguous colon concatenation); persist a hash of the tuple where key length would exceed 512 characters. Aggregate keys use canonical string IDs, a tenant and store/domain identity; legacy integer IDs are normalized to decimal strings. Tenant is always validated against authenticated/configured scope, never merely trusted from event JSON.
+
+Party display names can be personal data: event storage requires restricted access/retention, and payloads are never logged. No token, password, URI, OTP, mobile number, full address, credentials, raw provider body, report row or source XML may enter logs/metric labels. Proposed held-voucher events carry an encrypted evidence reference/digest only; the existing encrypted Tally task is not republished as plaintext. Provider order schemas carry only required identifiers/status/lines and eligibility; financial/customer details stay in restricted owner records until D05 defines an approved snapshot schema. `changed_fields`/digest metadata does not authorize copying an arbitrary object.
+
+At compatibility boundaries, an explicit adapter maps current task shapes to `.v1`; it must validate native identity and version, not relabel old JSON as a valid new event. Unknown major version or unapproved metadata module is held for review without an effect. Keep v1 readers until replay retention ends; incompatible changes produce v2 and a reviewed migration/replay strategy. Deleted masters use monotonic tombstones and cannot resurrect through a stale replay.
+
+## Ordering policies (proposed)
+
+- **O1 — ordered local aggregate effects.** Allocate a durable monotonically increasing sequence under the aggregate lock/version update in the same local transaction that writes state and event. Consumer records `(tenant, consumer, aggregate, last_sequence)` and dedupe receipt atomically with effects. Same event/digest is a no-op; same ID with different digest is a conflict; gaps wait or enter review, never leap into a physical delta. No global ordering is promised. Cross-aggregate multi-line orders lock identities in sorted order as `inventory/workers.js` already attempts; transfers have an explicit transaction/group identity so both legs are one effect. Receipt command and result are different local streams tied by request/digest, not an assumed global sequence shared between stores.
+- **O_PROVIDER — provider snapshot ordering.** Receipt sequence preserves local arrival order only. Compare an approved provider revision/UTC timestamp per account/order or mapping. Older snapshots cannot reverse terminal/physical effects. Equal revision with different content is review-required. Missing revision is retained as observational/unverified with `stock_eligible=false`; do not invent order using ingestion time. Partial shipment needs explicit line/package identities and quantities before consume/release. Report order requires approved export timestamp plus digest; stock reports lacking source ordering remain held, not last-write-wins.
+- **O_LATEST — latest desired-state publishing/projection.** Process per mapping/identity with a lock; re-read current approved mapping and ledger version. Coalesce stale publish requests into the current target, never roll a channel back to an old balance. Record which balance and mapping revision was actually submitted/observed. Unknown provider outcome stays uncertain until readback; submission is separate from confirmation. Read models may ignore lower source revisions but must process deletes and expose last-confirmed revision/staleness.
+- **O_DELIVERY — monotonic notification receipts.** Correlate configured tenant + sender + provider message ID. Advance accepted→sent→delivered→read; retain failed/cancelled/uncertain terminal evidence according to approved transition rules, and never let an older receipt regress delivered/read. Receipt dedupe includes provider message/status. An external receipt cannot select another tenant's row.
+
+The current `ci_outbox.id`, task claim `ORDER BY id`, provider timestamps or UI `at` fields do not implement O1. Even with single-worker advisory ownership, retries can run after later available tasks; durable aggregate sequencing still needs implementation and tests. Poll checkpoints must preserve overlap and a safe provider high-water mark established before the scan; resuming after failure must replay without skipping or duplicating business effects.
+
+## Retry policies (proposed)
+
+| ID | Contract | Existing evidence / difference |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| R1 | Internal effect: at most 8 business attempts, exponential backoff capped at 1 hour with randomized jitter; durable next-attempt time/lease/claim fencing; after limit persist DLQ/review before optional diagnostics. Retry only transient errors; schema, mapping, digest and business invariant failures held immediately. Aggregate poison blocks its own dependent sequence, never the entire account/tenant. | `inventory/workers.js:249-292` has attempts/2-minute lease/fencing/retry/dead and safe-state-before-diagnostics; backoff `min(3600,2**attempts*5)` is deterministic and no aggregate sequence consumer exists. |
+| R_EXTERNAL | Ingress persists receipt + local state + outbox in transaction, then acknowledges provider/SQS. Unavailable owner store produces retryable failure. Redelivery is accepted only for same ID/digest. Isolate individual queue poison messages; use durable validated quarantine and source DLQ visibility rules, never acknowledge before durable ownership transfer. | `ci_ingress` provides source receipts; `inventory/sqs-worker.js` deletes after acceptance but lacks per-message poison isolation. Provider retry schedules/queue configuration are NOT VERIFIED. |
+| R_PROVIDER | Stock writes follow R1 only with idempotent desired state/compare-and-set and current mapping. Respect provider quota and Retry-After; add jitter; 429/5xx retry, auth disables/quarantines account until renewal/retest, hard validation held. Ambiguous response gets readback/reconciliation before repeat; never count submitted as observed. | `inventory/rate-limit.js`, connector wrappers and `publishStockTask` supply partial controls; no claim that every provider supports idempotency/readback. |
+| R_ALERT | One initial actionable effect per order/recipient. Current proven rejection that is retryable may retry up to 8 with jitter/cap; known auth/template/policy/freshness failure is terminal. Once request may have left process and outcome is unknown, mark uncertain and require provider evidence/operator review. No automatic duplicate send after ambiguous outcome. Explicit operator requeue must acknowledge duplication risk and be audited. | `inventory/whatsapp.js:82-96,98-165` and `inventory/alert-admin.js:103-113` implement parts; cross-path one-per-recipient policy pending D07. One-second sender lock currently bounds attempts to roughly 1/sec per tenant. |
+| R_HELD | No automatic business execution. Retain source receipt/diagnostic; require approved mapping/semantics and audited replay decision, preserving original id/digest. | `tally_voucher_review` is held; unknown/partial stock semantics must remain held. |
+| R_REFRESH | UI invalidation hint only. On disconnect/missing hint, reload authoritative state on reconnect/focus and timed poll; no business retry or effect. Expose stale/last-confirmed state. | Current SSE + hook fallback provides hints, not a durable event bus. |
+
+Retention/dedupe must outlive allowed provider redelivery and operational replay. Draft: retain event/outbox/consumer receipts for 90 days, configurable per legal/PII rules (D11); do not prune receipts while matching business effects can be replayed. A separate compact business-effect key survives longer if records are archived. Request key reuse with changed content is rejected, never treated as a new command.
+
+## Proposed transaction topology: no cross-store dual write
+
+Mongo originating writers (`api/skus.js`, dedicated masters and inbound APIs) commit their local entity revision and **PROPOSED Mongo `_domain_outbox`** in one replica-set transaction. A dispatcher reads this durable collection; change streams may wake it up only if the actual tier supports the needed features (Phase B documentation/owner-fact gate). Poll/reconciliation always remains the safety net. Change-stream resume token must be stored durably; no `watch()`/resume-token implementation was found in the inspected source. No Atlas capability is presumed from driver code.
+
+For a receipt command, a PostgreSQL consumer first validates the version/tenant/source identity/digest and approved PO/SKU/location quantity contract, then commits **PROPOSED `ci_event_receipt` + ledger changes + existing `ci_outbox` carrying the receipt-result envelope** in one PG transaction. `ci_event_receipt` is a proposed general consumer dedupe table, not the existing source-specific `ci_ingress`. Its logical unique key is `(tenant_id, consumer_name, event_id)` plus digest, aggregate key/sequence and effect reference; sequence checkpoint is transactionally fenced. A duplicate cannot repeat stock; failed validation records a held/rejected result without successful ledger effects.
+
+A Mongo receipt-result consumer commits **PROPOSED `_domain_inbox` + GRN projection/revision** in a Mongo transaction. It cannot generate stock. Projection update uses expected request/digest/revision; changed identity or missing command is a review conflict. Lost acknowledgements replay original envelopes. These proposed collections/tables are named for review only: none was created. The general PG domain-event task router, Mongo dispatcher and projector are not current `runInventoryTaskBatch` features; Phase B must implement and prove them after approval.
+
+PO limits need their own approval/claim version: checking mutable Mongo PO quantity during a PG transaction cannot guarantee a stable cross-store limit. D08 must choose an immutable approved receipt authorization (per-PO/SKU cumulative budget/claim with monotonically owned revisions) carried to the PG consumer. The command schema's `max_receivable_quantity` is an authorization ceiling, not permission to trust arbitrary UI numbers; the consumer verifies issuer/identity/revision and cumulative posted quantities before posting. Changes to a PO after authorization cannot retract already committed receipt facts silently.
+
+For PG-originated effects, business state, downstream envelope and dedupe/checkpoint remain in the PG transaction. Retrying provider sends remains an external side-effect boundary: no distributed transaction or unsupported exactly-once promise is made. State-driven publication can converge through idempotent writes/readback; notification uncertainty requires explicit review. Return/transfer consumers preserve the same request digest and exact approved identities, and legacy projections cannot initiate a second receipt/dispatch.
+
+## Required proof in Phase B (NOT RUN here)
+
+Fresh `_test` replica-set Mongo + isolated PG; prove entity+outbox atomicity by abort/crash, event/digest conflict rejection, duplicate consumer delivery, sequence gap/out-of-order handling, multi-line rollback, restart after claim/commit/ack, dispatch retry/poison isolation, durable cursor/resume token recovery, stale mapping/balance behavior, and Mongo→PG→Mongo receipt replay without a second physical movement. Record p50/p95/p99 with endpoint/UI version visibility against ADR-003. Existing tests are candidate starting points, not evidence until safely rerun at that scope.
